Remote work and freelancing for overseas clients is now a normal part of Sri Lankan working life, and the tax treatment of that income has its own rules worth understanding before you file. Worker remittances into Sri Lanka hit a record **USD 8.076 billion in 2025**, up 22.8% from the year before, and industry voices increasingly point out that official IT/BPM export figures (USD 1.65 billion in 2025) understate the real picture, because a growing freelance community gets paid through personal remittance channels that don't show up in formal export statistics.

![A hand holding a fan of foreign banknotes against a teal background](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1580048915913-4f8f5cb481c4?q=80&w=1600&auto=format&fit=crop)

## The foreign-service concession: 15%, but only if you remit through a bank

Since 1 April 2025, under the **Inland Revenue (Amendment) Act No. 2 of 2025**, a specific category of income qualifies for a concessionary **maximum 15% tax rate** instead of the ordinary progressive bands. The Act's own wording is precise about what qualifies:

> "The gains and profits earned or derived from any service rendered in or outside Sri Lanka to any person to be utilized outside Sri Lanka, where the payment for such services is received in foreign currency and remitted through a bank to Sri Lanka."

Two conditions, both required: the service is rendered to someone outside Sri Lanka (or the benefit is used outside Sri Lanka), and the payment is received in foreign currency and remitted through a Sri Lankan bank. Miss either one and the concession doesn't apply.

## What happens if you don't remit through a bank

This is where the concession quietly disappears. If the same foreign-service income isn't remitted through the banking system (paid into a foreign account you never bring home, received via a payment method that doesn't route through a licensed bank, or simply not tracked properly), it falls back to being taxed under the **ordinary progressive bands**, which top out at **36%**. The gap between 15% and 36% on the same underlying income is the single biggest reason this rule is worth understanding before, not after, you've already spent the money.

| | Qualifies for 15% concession | Doesn't qualify |
|---|---|---|
| Condition | Foreign currency, remitted through a Sri Lankan bank | Not remitted through a bank, or service used within Sri Lanka |
| Tax treatment | Maximum 15% | Ordinary progressive bands, up to 36% |
| Where it's set out | Inland Revenue (Amendment) Act No. 2 of 2025, First Schedule | Standard Inland Revenue Act bands |

*Source: [Inland Revenue (Amendment) Act No. 2 of 2025, Parliament of Sri Lanka](https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/acts/gbills/english/6379.pdf); [IRD Notice to Taxpayers PN/IT/2025-01](https://www.ird.gov.lk/en/Lists/Latest%20News%20%20Notices/Attachments/666/PN_IT_2025-01_26032025_E.pdf).*

## Foreign tax credits: relief for tax already paid overseas, with a real cap

If a platform or client withholds tax in their own country before paying you, Sri Lanka doesn't tax that same income again from scratch. **Section 80 of the Inland Revenue Act No. 24 of 2017** lets a resident individual claim a foreign tax credit for foreign income tax already paid on their assessable foreign income.

But the credit isn't unlimited. **Section 81(1)(b)** caps it at the Sri Lankan tax that would otherwise be payable on that same income. Two practical outcomes follow directly from that cap:

- If the foreign tax you paid is **less** than your Sri Lankan liability on that income, you pay the **difference** to the IRD. The credit doesn't wipe the bill out; it just prevents double taxation up to what Sri Lanka would have charged.
- If the foreign tax you paid is **more** than your Sri Lankan liability, **Section 81(3)** is explicit that the excess is not refunded, and cannot be carried back or carried forward to another year. It's simply lost.

That second point catches people by surprise more than the first, because there's no "banking" a foreign tax credit for later use. Whatever isn't used against this year's Sri Lankan liability on this year's foreign income doesn't survive past this year's return.

## The mistakes that cost people the most

The two most common: not remitting through a bank in a way that qualifies for the 15% concession (turning a 15% bill into one taxed up to 36% on the exact same income), and not keeping documentation of foreign tax paid, which makes it hard to substantiate a Section 80 credit claim at all. Both mean paying more than necessary, on money you already earned honestly.

Salli's engine applies these rules from your actual ledger entries, checking whether income was remitted through a bank in a way that qualifies, and tracking foreign tax paid so a Section 80 credit isn't missed, so the qualifying treatment isn't something you have to remember to claim yourself.

